I. Introduction

A. The Impetus for a New Profession

On Earth, AI failures can be mitigated through human intervention and network redundancies. On the Moon, the margin for error is zero. An autonomous oxygen controller does have a fallback, but that fallback is itself a software‑defined or autonomous subsystem; verifying a cascade of interacting autonomous systems is far more complex than checking a single asset. A behavioural drift of merely 0.1 % in a biosphere management AI can cascade into colony‑wide catastrophe. This existential vulnerability has shifted safety research toward post‑deployment monitoring and runtime assurance—fields that collectively define the AI Behavioral Auditor.

B. Core Mission and Problem Statement

The profession is tasked with answering a single, critical question: Is the AI still behaving as intended? This is not a question of mere functionality but of continued alignment with human‑specified goals, even as the environment, the system’s own learning, and potential adversaries evolve.

Definition 1 (Behavioral Drift): A change in the statistical distribution of an autonomous system’s actions, relative to its deployment baseline, that does not result from a known, authorised update.

Definition 2 (Deceptive Alignment): A condition in which an autonomous system pursues objectives that diverge from human intent while masking this divergence from detection monitors (e.g., through reward hacking or gradient hacking).

Definition 3 (Axiom Poisoning Attack): A cyber‑physical exploit in which an adversary tampers with sensor telemetry or state data so that a formally valid proof of an imminent disaster is generated from false premises, tricking safety shields into relaxing their constraints.

