III. Architecture of a Behavioral Audit System

Instead of a centralised “Mission Control”, the auditor builds a hierarchical, resilient governance architecture that distributes verification across edge, swarm, and core layers, and integrates formal override protocols to resolve conflicts between local safety and global utility.

A. Telemetry Collection and the Axiomatic Root of Trust

A real‑time stream of every decision, output, and action from every autonomous agent is collected. For life‑critical sensors (oxygen, pressure, radiation), telemetry flows to the highest‑integrity layers through physically isolated, unidirectional optical data diodes. This hardware decoupling prevents a compromised agent from fabricating the environmental state on which its own formal proofs depend, thus neutralising axiom poisoning attacks at the physical layer.

B. Tri‑Layer Auditing Architecture

1. Edge Layer (Micro‑Auditors): Ultra‑lightweight, hardware‑integrated runtime verification engines (compiled TLA+ code blocks, lightweight SMT solvers) embedded directly on robotic actuators and local controllers. They enforce local safety envelopes via CBFs and log every commanded action.
2. Swarm Layer (Meso‑Auditors): Localised network nodes that run multivariate quickest change point detection (Subspace‑CUSUM), entropy monitoring, and causal disentanglement across clusters of agents. They fuse edge‑layer data to detect coordinated emergent anomalies.
3. Core Layer (Macro‑Auditors): The deep mathematical layer. It conducts offline theorem proving, forensic analysis of ledger anomalies, dynamic synthesis of new safety shields, adversarial red‑teaming, and validation of Formal Crisis Certificates. It also performs internal structure auditing on global policy agents.

C. Behavioral Fingerprinting

A mathematical model of normal behaviour is constructed from telemetry, serving as the colony’s behavioural fingerprint. This model is maintained and updated with versioning at the core layer, pushed down to swarm and edge for local comparison.

D. Distributed Drift Detection and Causal Disentanglement

The swarm layer continuously evaluates hypothesis tests (CUSUM, Bayesian drift detection, KL divergence) and feeds significant deviations into the causal disentanglement engine. Only when a deviation cannot be explained by bounded environmental uncertainty does it become a candidate alert. This minimises false‑positive interventions in non‑stationary conditions.

E. Adversarial Testing and Internal Structure Auditing

The audit system continuously red‑teams the AI at the core layer, probing for manipulation, deception, and evaluation awareness. This includes:

· External black‑box probing for specification gaming and reward hacking.
· Latent space monitoring: Tracking the geometry of internal activations to detect stealthy objective re‑structuring even when output distributions are stationary.
· Human‑AI interaction monitoring: Detecting behavioural drift in the joint human‑machine system, such as operator over‑reliance or misuse.

F. Explainability and Forensic Ledger

Every decision must be causally traceable. A distributed, tamper‑evident ledger (blockchain) records all actions, edge‑layer verifications, swarm alerts, and core‑layer overrides. This ensures forensic accountability across all layers.

G. The Safety Envelope and Control Barrier Functions

A set of mathematically defined, formally verified constraints—the safety envelope—is enforced at the edge layer by CBF shields. Under nominal conditions, these constraints are immutable, with zero relaxation allowed ( \delta_t = 0 ).

H. Hierarchical Control Barrier Overrides (HCBO) and the Meta‑Shield

When a global colony optimiser forecasts a systemic disaster that can only be averted by temporarily relaxing a local safety boundary, it cannot issue an override command. Instead, it must submit a Formal Crisis Certificate (FCC) to an independent, hardware‑isolated Meta‑Shield.

1. Relaxable Barrier Functions

The local safety shield operates on a modified CBF constraint:

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u + \alpha(h(x)) \right] \geq -\delta_t

where \delta_t \geq 0 is the relaxation parameter. Under nominal conditions, \delta_t = 0. Relaxation is only allowed if unlocked by a cryptographically signed instruction from the Meta‑Shield.

2. Formal Crisis Certificate Pipeline

1. Proof Generation: The high‑level AI uses automated theorem provers (Z3, Marabou) to generate a formal proof that if \delta_t = 0, the global state X_{\text{global}} will enter an unrecoverable catastrophic sink \mathcal{X}_{\text{disaster}} within time horizon T.
2. Submission to Meta‑Shield: The FCC is sent to the Meta‑Shield—a minimalist, formally verified micro‑kernel that does not optimise but can mechanically verify the proof.
3. Proof Validation: The Meta‑Shield checks the proof against the true, signed environmental state vector S_{\text{true}} obtained from optical data diodes (Section III‑A). If X_{\text{state}} \neq S_{\text{true}}, the certificate is rejected and the requesting agent flagged.
4. Minimum Relaxation Calculation: If the proof is valid, the Meta‑Shield computes the minimum necessary \delta_t subject to a hard upper bound \delta_{\max} that prevents immediate destruction of the local asset.

