Appendix A. Probabilistic Drift Detection: CUSUM and Subspace‑CUSUM

A.1 Statistical Model

Let  X_t \in \mathcal{X}  be the vector of observable behavioural outputs of an agent at time  t . Under nominal operation, the sequence  \{X_t\}  is assumed to be independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) according to a known pre‑change distribution  F_0 . At an unknown change point  \tau , the distribution switches to  F_1 . The auditor tests

H_0 : \tau > t \quad \text{vs} \quad H_1 : \tau \le t .

The log‑likelihood ratio for a single observation is

\ell(X_t) = \log \frac{f_1(X_t)}{f_0(X_t)},

where  f_0, f_1  are densities with respect to a common dominating measure.

A.2 Cumulative Sum (CUSUM) Procedure

The CUSUM statistic  W_t  is defined recursively by

W_0 = 0,\qquad W_t = \max\{0,\, W_{t-1} + \ell(X_t)\}.

An alarm is raised at the stopping time

N = \inf\{ t \ge 1 : W_t \ge h \},

where  h > 0  is a threshold chosen to control the average run length under  H_0 . For a desired false‑alarm rate  \alpha , the threshold can be set using the approximation

\mathbb{E}_0[N] \approx \frac{e^{h} - h - 1}{| \mathbb{E}_0[\ell] |},

valid when  \mathbb{E}_0[\ell] < 0  (Lorden 1971). In practice,  h  is calibrated via Monte Carlo simulation on baseline telemetry.

A.3 Subspace‑CUSUM for Multivariate Agent Ensembles

For a cluster of  m  agents, the auditor monitors a matrix  \mathbf{X}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times d} . The Subspace‑CUSUM procedure (adapted from Mei 2010, Xie et al. 2013) projects the observation onto a low‑dimensional subspace that captures the direction of the fault. Let  U \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times r}  be an orthonormal matrix defining the monitored subspace. The projected score is

S_t = \| U^\top X_t \|_2^2 - \beta,

where  \beta  is a pre‑shift baseline. The CUSUM recursion is applied to  S_t  with a suitable log‑likelihood ratio derived from the chi‑squared distribution of the projected norm under  H_0  and  H_1 . This yields quick detection of coordinated anomalies while suppressing noise in irrelevant dimensions.

