Appendix B. Safety Invariance via Control Barrier Functions

B.1 Control Barrier Functions (CBFs)

Consider a control‑affine dynamical system

\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x) u, \quad x \in \mathbb{R}^n, \; u \in \mathcal{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^m,

with  f  and  g  locally Lipschitz. Let a closed safe set  \mathcal{C} \subset \mathbb{R}^n  be defined as the 0‑superlevel set of a continuously differentiable function  h : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R} :

\mathcal{C} = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}^n : h(x) \ge 0 \}, \quad \partial\mathcal{C} = \{ x : h(x) = 0 \}.

Definition B.1 (Control Barrier Function, Ames et al. 2017). The function  h  is a Control Barrier Function on a set  \mathcal{D}  with  \mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathcal{D} \subset \mathbb{R}^n  if there exists an extended class‑ \mathcal{K}  function  \alpha  such that for all  x \in \mathcal{D} ,

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u + \alpha(h(x)) \right] \ge 0,
\tag{B.1}

where  L_f h = \nabla h \cdot f  and  L_g h = \nabla h \cdot g  are Lie derivatives.

B.2 Forward Invariance Guarantee

Theorem B.1 (Safety). If  h  is a CBF on  \mathcal{D}  and a locally Lipschitz control policy  u = k(x)  satisfies

L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) k(x) + \alpha(h(x)) \ge 0 \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{D},

then the safe set  \mathcal{C}  is forward invariant:  x(0) \in \mathcal{C} \implies x(t) \in \mathcal{C}  for all  t \ge 0 .

Proof. Define  V(x) = -h(x) . Along trajectories,  \dot{V}(x) = - (L_f h + L_g h u) . From the inequality,  L_f h + L_g h u \ge -\alpha(h(x)) = \alpha(-V(x)) . Hence

\dot{V}(x) \le -\alpha(-V(x)) = -\alpha(V(x)) \quad (\text{since } \alpha \text{ is odd for class‑}\mathcal{K}).

Because  \alpha  is positive definite,  \dot{V} \le -\alpha(V)  implies that  V  remains non‑positive if initially non‑positive; hence  h(x(t)) \ge 0  for all  t . ∎

B.3 Runtime Safety Shield Implementation

At each control cycle, the edge‑layer micro‑auditor solves a quadratic program (QP) minimally modifying the proposed AI action  u_{\text{AI}}  to satisfy the CBF constraint (B.1) while respecting actuation limits. The existence of a feasible solution is guaranteed by the CBF condition. This yields a formally verified safety shield that does not require knowledge of the AI’s internal state.

