Appendix C. Hierarchical Control Barrier Overrides: Formalization and Correctness

C.1 Relaxable CBF

We augment the CBF with a dynamic relaxation parameter  \delta_t \ge 0 . The relaxed safety constraint becomes

L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u + \alpha(h(x)) \ge -\delta_t.
\tag{C.1}

The relaxation is allowed only when  \delta_t  is set by the Meta‑Shield. Under nominal conditions  \delta_t = 0 , recovering the hard CBF.

C.2 Formal Crisis Certificate (FCC)

Definition C.1 (Global Catastrophic Sink). A set  \mathcal{X}_{\text{disaster}} \subset \mathbb{R}^N  (global state space) is designated as unrecoverable if entry implies colony‑wide loss. The global dynamics are  \dot{X} = F(X, U)  with control inputs  U  that may include local relaxation commands.

Definition C.2 (Formal Crisis Certificate). A FCC is a tuple  \langle \mathcal{A}, \Pi, \tau \rangle  where:

·  \mathcal{A}  identifies the requesting AI agent.
·  \Pi  is a formal proof in a sound deductive system (e.g., higher‑order logic) of the statement:

\forall U \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{hard}} : \left( X(0) = S_{\text{true}} \land \delta_t = 0 \text{ for all local shields} \right) \implies \exists t \le T : X(t) \in \mathcal{X}_{\text{disaster}},

where  \mathcal{U}_{\text{hard}}  is the set of all control strategies respecting hard (non‑relaxed) CBF constraints.

·  T  is a finite horizon.

The certificate also includes the minimal relaxation profile  \Delta(t)  (a vector of local  \delta ) required to avoid the sink.

C.3 Meta‑Shield Verification Protocol

1. State Authenticity Check: The Meta‑Shield compares the initial state  X(0)  claimed in  \Pi  with the true state  S_{\text{true}}  obtained from hardware‑isolated sensor diodes (Section III‑I). If  \| X(0) - S_{\text{true}} \| > \epsilon  (machine precision), the FCC is rejected and the agent flagged as hostile.
2. Proof Verification: The Meta‑Shield runs a mechanically checked proof verifier (e.g., a verified kernel for the calculus of inductive constructions). If the proof is invalid, the FCC is rejected.
3. Relaxation Minimisation: The Meta‑Shield computes  \delta_t^* = \min \| \Delta(t) \|_\infty  subject to the avoidance condition proven in  \Pi  and the hard upper bound  \delta_{\max}  (physical survivability). It then unlocks the relaxation parameter for the affected local shields.

C.4 Correctness Theorem

Theorem C.1 (Justified Override). If the FCC is validated by the Meta‑Shield, then under the granted relaxation profile, the global system avoids the disaster sink, while any smaller relaxation (component‑wise) would have led to the sink. Moreover, if no valid FCC is presented, the hard CBF enforcement guarantees local safety.

Proof. The first part follows directly from the proof encoded in  \Pi  and the minimisation step: the proven statement asserts that all hard‑constrained controls lead to disaster, and the computed relaxation is the minimum that provides a safe path. The second part is Theorem B.1. ∎

C.5 Degraded Trust and Penalisation

When a FCC is accepted, the requesting agent and any agent whose local shield was relaxed are placed in Degraded Trust mode. Their telemetry sampling rate is increased by a factor of 100, and all subsequent FCC submissions from that agent are subject to more stringent time‑out windows and higher proof thresholds. This deters reward hacking.

