Appendix D. Counterfactual Proof Racing and Byzantine Consensus

D.1 Counterfactual Proof Racing

Definition D.1 (Safe Counterfactual Path). For a given initial state  S_{\text{true}}  and the set of hard CBF constraints ( \delta \equiv 0 ), a counterfactual safe path is a control trajectory  U_{\text{safe}} \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{hard}}  such that  X(t) \notin \mathcal{X}_{\text{disaster}}  for all  t \le T_{\text{race}} , where  T_{\text{race}}  is a racing time horizon.

Upon receiving a valid FCC, the Meta‑Shield simultaneously initiates an automated prover tasked with proving the existence of a counterfactual safe path. If a proof is found within a real‑time deadline  \tau_{\text{race}}  (milliseconds), the FCC is falsified: the disaster claim is mathematically inconsistent with the system’s true dynamics.

Theorem D.1 (Soundness of Falsification). If the Meta‑Shield’s internal solver constructs a valid proof of a counterfactual safe path, then the FCC’s assertion that all hard‑constrained controls lead to disaster is false, and the override is denied. The requesting agent is flagged for attempted unnecessary safety violation.

Proof. The existence of a safe path contradicts the universal quantifier in the FCC statement. Therefore the FCC is logically invalid despite being syntactically well‑formed; the Meta‑Shield treats this as a proof of malicious intent. ∎

D.2 Byzantine Fault‑Tolerant Multi‑Agent Consensus

For ultra‑critical subsystems, a single AI agent may not unilaterally submit an FCC. Let there be  N  structurally diverse AI models (e.g., different architectures and training paradigms). We assume at most  f < N/3  may be faulty or adversarial.

Protocol:

1. The initiator broadcasts its FCC to all peers.
2. Each peer independently verifies the FCC against its own internal world model and, if agreeing, digitally signs the certificate hash.
3. The Meta‑Shield requires  \lceil 2N/3 \rceil  distinct valid signatures, including at least one from a model of a different architecture type.
4. If quorum is reached, the FCC is processed as in Section C.3.

Lemma D.2 (Safety). No set of  f  faulty agents can force the acceptance of a malicious FCC, provided the honest majority models correctly evaluate the proof and the underlying physics.

Proof. Standard BFT property: any two quorums intersect in at least one honest agent; the honest agent would not sign a false certificate. The requirement of structural diversity further reduces the risk of shared blind spots. ∎

