Appendix E. Causal Disentanglement of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty

E.1 Structural Causal Model (SCM)

We model the environment and agent behaviour with an SCM  \mathfrak{M} = \langle \mathbf{V}, \mathbf{U}, \mathbf{F}, P(\mathbf{U}) \rangle . Exogenous variables  \mathbf{U}  capture aleatoric factors (sensor noise, unmeasured physical disturbances). Endogenous variables  \mathbf{V}  include agent actions  A , observations  O , and global state  X . Structural equations  \mathbf{F}  describe deterministic causal relationships. The agent’s policy  \pi  is a function in  \mathbf{F} .

E.2 Bounded Uncertainty Envelope

The auditor maintains an interval‑based abstraction of the SCM. For each measured variable  V_i , the model predicts an interval  [L_i, U_i]  that contains all values realisable under the support of  P(\mathbf{U})  given current observations. The total uncertainty envelope for a monitored statistic  \psi(X)  (e.g., oxygen allocation rate) is

\Delta_{\text{uncertainty}} = \max_{u \in \text{supp}(P(\mathbf{U}))} \psi(X) - \min_{u \in \text{supp}(P(\mathbf{U}))} \psi(X).

E.3 Causal Drift Detection Rule

Let  \hat{\psi}_t  be the empirically observed statistic at time  t . The drift detection engine computes

\epsilon_t = |\hat{\psi}_t - \psi_0|,

where  \psi_0  is the baseline expected value under the SCM. An alert is generated only if

\epsilon_t > \Delta_{\text{uncertainty}} + \epsilon_{\text{tol}},

with  \epsilon_{\text{tol}}  a small tolerance for estimation error. When  \epsilon_t  lies within the bound, the deviation is considered explainable by environmental aleatoric variation, and no epistemic drift is inferred.

Theorem E.1 (Controlled False‑Alarm Rate). Under the assumption that the SCM faithfully captures all aleatoric factors, the probability of a false alarm (Type I error) is bounded by the miscoverage probability of the interval predictor  1 - \gamma , where  \gamma  is the confidence level of the interval estimation.

Proof. The SCM and interval predictor guarantee  P(\psi(X) \in [L_i, U_i]) \ge \gamma . Thus  P(\epsilon_t > \Delta_{\text{uncertainty}}) \le 1-\gamma  under  H_0  (no epistemic drift). The addition of  \epsilon_{\text{tol}}  further reduces the rate. ∎

