Appendix F. Compositional Verification and Assume‑Guarantee Reasoning

F.1 Framework

A multi‑agent system is a parallel composition of components  M_1 \parallel M_2 \parallel \dots \parallel M_k . Each component  M_i  interacts with its environment (other components and physical world) through input/output ports. A component specification is a pair  (A_i, G_i)  where:

·  A_i  is an assumption about the behaviour of  M_i ’s environment.
·  G_i  is a guarantee promised by  M_i  when  A_i  holds.

We write  M_i \models \langle A_i \rangle \langle G_i \rangle  to mean that  M_i  satisfies its guarantee under the assumption.

F.2 Compositional Proof Rule

The classical assume‑guarantee rule (Misra & Chandy 1981, Abadi & Lamport 1993) states:

\frac{ M_1 \models \langle A_1 \rangle \langle G_1 \rangle \quad \cdots \quad M_k \models \langle A_k \rangle \langle G_k \rangle \quad
   A \land \bigwedge_{i} G_i \implies A }
{ \|_{i} M_i \models \langle A \rangle \langle \bigwedge_i G_i \rangle }

where  A  is the global environment assumption. The circularity of assumptions (each  A_i  may be implied by the guarantees of others) is valid provided the logic is suitably formulated.

F.3 Application to Lunar Colony Edge Shields

Each edge‑layer micro‑auditor enforces a local CBF guarantee  G_i : "the agent’s action maintains  h_i(x) \ge 0  under the assumption that the physical plant behaves according to the nominal dynamics." The swarm layer aggregates these guarantees to infer network‑wide safety. Compositional verification avoids the state‑space explosion of a monolithic global model, reducing the verification task to  k  sub‑problems of size independent of the total number of agents.

