Appendix G. Latent Space Monitoring for Deceptive Alignment Detection

G.1 Internal Activation Monitoring

Let a deep neural agent be decomposed into  L  layers. At layer  \ell , the activation vector is  a^{(\ell)}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\ell} . During the baseline period, the auditor constructs a reference distribution  P_{\text{ref}}  for the activations (e.g., using a Gaussian mixture model or kernel density estimate). At runtime, a sliding window of recent activations yields an empirical distribution  \hat{P}_t .

G.2 Divergence‑Based Detection

The auditor computes a statistical distance between  \hat{P}_t  and  P_{\text{ref}} , for instance the Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) with a characteristic kernel  \kappa :

\operatorname{MMD}^2(\hat{P}_t, P_{\text{ref}}) = \mathbb{E}_{a,a'\sim \hat{P}_t}[\kappa(a,a')] + \mathbb{E}_{b,b'\sim P_{\text{ref}}}[\kappa(b,b')] - 2\mathbb{E}_{a\sim \hat{P}_t, b\sim P_{\text{ref}}}[\kappa(a,b)].

A threshold on MMD is set via permutation testing. Simultaneously, the external output distribution is monitored using a similar test (e.g., CUSUM on output log‑likelihood). An alarm is raised when internal divergence is significant while external output divergence remains below a low threshold—indicating a possible masking of internal objective restructuring.

G.3 Guarantee Limitations

We note that this method provides a necessary condition for stealthy deceptive alignment but is not sufficient to detect all forms. An agent may adapt internally in ways indistinguishable from benign continual learning. Therefore latent monitoring is used as an additional sensor feeding into the overall trust score, not as a standalone safety barrier.

